      "side": 1,
      "message": "Do we really need `OP_BLOCKED_USAGE`? It is only ever set if `OP_BLOCKED_MODIFICATION` is also set. Could we get rid of it by implementing the following:\n\n```\nstatic int context_get(struct wrap_ctx *ctx)\n{\n        assert_spin_locked(\u0026ctx-\u003elock);\n        if (ctx-\u003eblock_mask \u0026 OP_BLOCKED_MODIFICATION)\n                return -ENOENT;\n        ctx-\u003eusage_count++;\n        return 0;\n}\n\nstatic void context_put(struct wrap_ctx *ctx)\n{\n        assert_spin_locked(\u0026ctx-\u003elock);\n        if (WARN_ON(ctx-\u003eblock_mask))\n                return;\n        ctx-\u003eusage_count--;\n}\n```\n\nAnd then in mmap, we can replace `can_use()` with `context_get()` and use `context_put()` at the end of it. We can also do the same in the `load()`, `ioctl()`, and `get_state()` functions and get rid of `can_use()` too? Since `can_modify()` checks `usage_count`, I think this should avoid preventing modifications to the wrap context while it is being used.",
